# CSS Best Practices

These rules enforce CSS architecture best practices across the web application.

## 1. Component Classes Required for UI Controls

All reusable UI controls **must** use CSS component classes defined in `web/src/styles/globals.css`.

### Required Component Classes

**Buttons:**
- Primary actions → `.btn-primary`
- Secondary actions → `.btn-secondary`
- Tertiary/ghost actions → `.btn-ghost`
- Icon buttons → `.btn-icon`
- Search buttons → `.btn-search`
- Call buttons → `.btn-call`
- CTA buttons → `.cta-button`
- Location buttons → `.btn-location-primary` or `.btn-location-link`
- Mobile menu toggle → `.btn-menu-toggle`
- Close buttons → `.btn-close`
- Footer CTAs → `.btn-footer-cta`

**Links:**
- Navigation links → `.link-nav`
- Footer links → `.link-footer`
- Text/inline links → `.link-text`
- Mobile menu links → `.link-mobile`
- State directory links → `.link-state`
- Location chips/pills → `.link-location-chip`

**Inputs:**
- Standard inputs → `.input`
- Search inputs → `.input-search`
- Large search → `.input-search-lg`
- Transparent (expandable search) → `.input-transparent`

**Cards:**
- Base card → `.card`
- Hoverable card → `.card-hover`
- Feature card → `.card-feature`

**Badges:**
- Promo → `.badge-promo`
- Distance → `.badge-distance`
- Tag → `.badge-tag`

**Labels:**
- Form labels → `.label`

### Examples

```tsx
// ✅ CORRECT: Using component classes
<button className="btn-primary">Submit</button>
<Link to="/about" className="link-nav">About</Link>
<input type="text" className="input-search" />
<div className="card-hover">Content</div>

// ❌ WRONG: Inline Tailwind for controls
<button className="bg-brand-red-600 text-white px-4 py-2 rounded-lg hover:bg-brand-red-700">
  Submit
</button>
```

## 2. Component Class Implementation

When creating components that wrap UI controls, map variants to component classes:

```tsx
// ✅ CORRECT
export default function Button({ variant = 'primary', ...props }) {
  const variantClasses = {
    primary: 'btn-primary',
    secondary: 'btn-secondary',
    ghost: 'btn-ghost',
  }
  
  return <button className={variantClasses[variant]} {...props} />
}

// ❌ WRONG
export default function Button({ variant = 'primary', ...props }) {
  const baseClasses = 'px-4 py-2 rounded-lg...'
  const variantClasses = {
    primary: 'bg-brand-red-600 text-white hover:bg-brand-red-700',
    // ...
  }
  
  return <button className={`${baseClasses} ${variantClasses[variant]}`} {...props} />
}
```

## 3. Combining Component Classes with Utilities

Component classes can be combined with Tailwind utilities for layout and spacing:

```tsx
// ✅ CORRECT: Component class + utility classes
<button className="btn-primary mt-4 ml-2">Submit</button>
<div className="card flex items-center gap-4">Content</div>

// ✅ CORRECT: Utilities for layout, component classes for controls
<div className="flex items-center gap-4">
  <button className="btn-primary">Submit</button>
  <button className="btn-secondary">Cancel</button>
</div>
```

## 4. When to Use Utilities vs Component Classes

### Use Component Classes For:
- Buttons, links, inputs, cards, badges, labels
- Any control defined in the design system
- Repeated style patterns (used 3+ times)

### Use Utility Classes For:
- Layout (flexbox, grid, positioning)
- Spacing (margins, padding, gaps)
- One-off customizations
- Responsive modifiers (`md:flex`, `lg:grid-cols-3`)
- Typography overrides in specific contexts
- Background/border colors for unique sections

## 5. Adding New Component Classes

Before adding new component classes:

1. **Check if it's truly reusable** - Will this exact pattern be used elsewhere?
2. **Consult the design system** - Is this pattern defined in `docs/design/design-system.md`?
3. **Define in globals.css** - Add to `@layer components` section
4. **Use `@apply` directive** - Compose from Tailwind utilities and design tokens
5. **Follow naming convention** - `{element}-{variant}`
6. **Document it** - Update `docs/design/css-architecture.md`

Example:

```css
/* web/src/styles/globals.css */
@layer components {
  .btn-new-variant {
    @apply inline-flex items-center justify-center gap-2 rounded-lg px-4 py-2;
    @apply bg-your-color text-your-text;
    @apply hover:bg-your-hover-color;
    @apply focus-visible:outline-none focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-focus-default;
  }
}
```

## 6. Naming Conventions

Component class names must follow this pattern:

```
{element}-{variant}
```

**Examples:**
- `btn-primary` (button, primary variant)
- `link-footer` (link, footer variant)
- `input-search` (input, search variant)
- `card-hover` (card, with hover state)
- `badge-promo` (badge, promotional variant)

**Special cases:**
- `.card` - Base card (no variant needed)
- `.cta-button` - Legacy special CTA (grandfathered in)

## 7. Component File Structure

Component files should be clean and focused on behavior:

```tsx
// ✅ CORRECT: Clean, readable, focused on behavior
import { ButtonHTMLAttributes, ReactNode } from 'react'

interface ButtonProps extends ButtonHTMLAttributes<HTMLButtonElement> {
  variant?: 'primary' | 'secondary' | 'ghost'
  children: ReactNode
}

export default function Button({ variant = 'primary', children, className = '', ...props }: ButtonProps) {
  const variantClasses = {
    primary: 'btn-primary',
    secondary: 'btn-secondary',
    ghost: 'btn-ghost',
  }

  return (
    <button className={`${variantClasses[variant]} ${className}`} {...props}>
      {children}
    </button>
  )
}
```

## 8. Forbidden Patterns

The following patterns are **not allowed**:

```tsx
// ❌ WRONG: Inline class concatenation for controls
const baseClasses = 'px-4 py-2 rounded-lg font-semibold transition-colors...'
const variantClasses = {
  primary: 'bg-brand-red-600 text-white hover:bg-brand-red-700',
}

// ❌ WRONG: Hardcoded color values
<button className="bg-[#C41F1D] text-white">Submit</button>

// ❌ WRONG: Recreating component classes inline
<a href="/about" className="text-sm text-black hover:text-brand-red-600 focus-visible:outline-none...">
  About
</a>
```

## 9. Design Token Usage

Component classes **must** use design tokens (colors, spacing, typography) via Tailwind's `@apply` directive or CSS custom properties:

```css
/* ✅ CORRECT: Using design tokens */
.btn-primary {
  @apply bg-brand-red-600 text-text-on-brand;
  @apply hover:bg-brand-red-700;
}

/* ✅ CORRECT: Using CSS custom properties */
.btn-primary {
  background-color: var(--color-brand-red-600);
  color: var(--color-text-on-brand);
}

/* ❌ WRONG: Hardcoded values */
.btn-primary {
  background-color: #C41F1D;
  color: #FFFFFF;
}
```

## 10. Accessibility Requirements

All component classes **must** include proper focus states:

```css
/* ✅ REQUIRED: Focus states for interactive elements */
.btn-primary {
  @apply focus-visible:outline-none focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-focus-default focus-visible:ring-offset-2;
}

.link-nav {
  @apply focus-visible:outline-none focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-focus-default;
}
```

## 11. Code Review Checklist

When reviewing CSS/component code, ensure:

- [ ] All buttons use `.btn-*` classes
- [ ] All links use `.link-*` classes
- [ ] All inputs use `.input-*` classes
- [ ] All cards use `.card*` classes
- [ ] No inline Tailwind class concatenation for controls
- [ ] Utilities are used only for layout and spacing
- [ ] Component classes are defined in `globals.css`
- [ ] New component classes follow naming conventions
- [ ] Focus states are present on interactive elements
- [ ] Design tokens are used (no hardcoded values)

